========================================================================================
                            AT Command Utility - Usage Guide
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 WARNING: Use with caution! If you are not sure what a command does DO NOT EXECUTE IT.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:
  atcmd [options] "AT-COMMAND"

Options:
  -a, --at             AT command
  -d, --delay <ms>     Delay in milliseconds (default: 600)
  -o, --output <file>  Save output (only /tmp/atcmd/)
  -n, --no-log         Disable syslog logging
  -c, --show-config    Print the configuration used by atcmd (config file in use,
                       imported values with their origin, fixed settings) and exit.
                       Read-only: does not touch the modem or the AT port.
                       Note: -c (lowercase) only prints; -C (uppercase) restarts
                       the modem. Cannot be combined with other actions.

  -S, --smart-qnwlock  Apply SMART QNWLOCK procedure. Accepts an
                       AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",... command as parameter;
                       behaviour depends on its value:
                       - Setting a lock (value 1-9): sent immediately, then
                         restarts modem via soft CFUN (AT+CFUN=0/1), restarts
                         WAN, and checks LTE connectivity. No preventive query.
                         If connectivity fails, the lock is removed and the restart is
                         repeated once.
                       - Removing a lock (value 0): first queries current
                         cell-lock status (AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"?); only if a
                         lock is actually active does it remove it and run the
                         same restart/WAN/connectivity sequence above.
                         If no lock is active: returns rc=13, no CFUN budget
                         consumed.
                         If the query gets no answer (it is retried once with a
                         longer delay): returns rc=12 and nothing is sent.

  -D, --at-diag        Run AT diagnostics snapshot (read-only, does not modify
                       modem state): AT+CEREG? (registration), AT+CGATT?
                       (GPRS attach), AT+CIMI (SIM/IMSI), AT+CGPADDR=1 (PDP
                       address). Does not accept additional AT commands.
                       Returns 0 if critical queries succeed, 1 if AT+CEREG?
                       or AT+CIMI fails/times out.

  -C, --smart-cfun     Execute SMART CFUN procedure (AT+CFUN=0 + AT+CFUN=1)
                       with modem restart, WAN restart, and LTE connectivity check.
                       Does not accept additional AT commands.
                       Preserves any existing cell-lock configuration.

  -Z, --sanity-check   Check modem is genuinely responsive (AT+CIMI)
                       Returns 0 if healthy, 1 if zombie state detected

  -h, --help, -help, help                      Show this help (brief usage)
  --help-full, -help-full, help-full, full     Show full documentation with examples

Notes:

  - If -a/--at is omitted, the first non-option argument is treated as the AT command.
  - atcmd -C exits with code 11 if the daily CFUN limit has been reached (14 AT+CFUN operations
    per 24h window by default; each modem restart uses 2).
  - The default AT port is /dev/ttyUSB2.
  - ttyport, cfun_daily_max, iface (WAN interface name) and ping_targets can be overridden in
    /tmp/watchdog/mbim.conf (runtime copy) or /etc/watchdog_mbim.conf, if present; otherwise the
    defaults at the top of the script are used.
  - This utility is not compatible with ModemManager.
  - Output files are restricted to /tmp/atcmd/ for safety reasons.
  - AT commands are blocked if /tmp/watchdog/atcmd_block exists.
  - Script exits with error (exit 5) if the interface defined in wan_iface
    is not found via ubus at startup (does not apply to -C, -S, -D, to
    AT+CFUN=* via -a, nor to AT+CGATT=1, AT+CGATT?, AT+CEREG?, AT+CPIN?, AT+COPS?
    and AT+COPS=0; see below). Set iface in /etc/watchdog_mbim.conf (or edit wan_iface at the top
    of the script) to match the LTE interface name defined in /etc/config/network.
  - AT+CFUN=* commands issued via -a bypass the wan_iface check above, since
    radio state is what determines interface presence, not the other way
    round. Two related safeguards apply:
      - If watchdog_mbim is running, the command is refused (exit 21) to avoid concurrent/uncoordinated
        recovery attempts. Stop watchdog_mbim first if you need to send AT+CFUN manually.
      - After a manual 'AT+CFUN=0' (radio OFF) + 'AT+CFUN=1' (radio ON) with watchdog_mbim stopped,
        WAN does NOT recover automatically: run 'ifup wan' manually afterwards,
        or use 'atcmd -C' instead, which restarts WAN and checks LTE connectivity as part of the same operation.

Warning:

  - Many AT commands modify persistent modem settings (NVRAM).
  - Some commands may cause loss of network connectivity or remote access.
  - Some commands require a longer delay (-d) to return complete output.
  - Commands that do not return 'OK' may be reported as failed.
  - Commands and responses are logged to syslog by default (use -n to disable).

Warning - special commands:
  - Using -S with AT+QNWLOCK setting a lock (or removing it while a lock is active) will restart the
    modem and restart WAN, then verify LTE automatically.
  - Using -C performs a soft CFUN reset (AT+CFUN=0/1), preserving any cell-lock configuration.
  - The CFUN operation counter is enforced daily to preserve modem flash lifespan.

Daily CFUN limit: 14 AT+CFUN operations per day by default (uptime-based 24h window, counted from
the first operation; one restart = 2 operations, a failed -C/-S run uses at least 4). Set
cfun_daily_max in /etc/watchdog_mbim.conf to change it (if /tmp/watchdog/mbim.conf exists it takes
precedence: remove it and restart watchdog_mbim). The counter lives in /tmp: it is cleared at reboot.
If the limit is reached right after AT+CFUN=0 (e.g. odd cfun_daily_max), AT+CFUN=1 is still sent
(not counted), so the radio is never left OFF.

Return codes:
  0  = success
  1  = generic error: invalid/missing argument (-a, -d, -o), unknown option,
       mutually exclusive flags used together, empty/invalid AT command,
       missing dependency (picocom/ubus/jsonfilter), usage file not found, AT command
       that did not return OK, or -Z/-D check failed
  2  = AT port ($ttyport) not found after ttyport_timeout (modem not present)
  3  = incompatible with ModemManager (proto 'modemmanager' found in config)
  4  = blocked: /tmp/watchdog/atcmd_block exists
  5  = wan_iface interface not found via ubus (does not apply to -C, -S, -D,
       AT+CFUN=* via -a and the read/re-attach queries bypassing this check -
       see Notes)
  6  = AT port already in use by another process
  7  = invalid -d/--delay value (must be numeric and > 0)
  8  = cannot create required directory, or -o/--output path invalid
       (must be inside /tmp/atcmd/ with no '..' components, parent directory must exist)
  9  = -S: invalid AT+QNWLOCK command format
  11 = daily CFUN limit reached, refused before any action was taken
       (pre-checked by -C only, before touching the modem)
  12 = critical failure during -S/-C (modem restart failed, or zombie state
       persists after restart and fallback attempts); also returned by -S
       if the AT+QNWLOCK query gets no answer after 2 attempts (nothing is sent,
       lock state unknown), and by -S
       if the daily CFUN limit is reached mid-procedure - in that case the
       requested AT+QNWLOCK command has already been sent (lock config may
       be updated) but the restart needed to apply it could not run
  13 = -S: no active cell-lock (fallback to atcmd -C recommended)
  20 = another instance is already running (lock directory exists)
  21 = manual AT+CFUN=* via -a refused because watchdog_mbim is running
       (stop watchdog_mbim first, then repeat the command)

Return code 13 (no cell-lock) is not a failure; it informs the watchdog that
removal is unnecessary and -C can be used directly to save CFUN budget.

Examples:

# Basic AT Commands
atcmd 'AT';           # Basic communication test (equivalent to: atcmd -a 'AT')
atcmd 'ATI';          # Show device information (equivalent to: atcmd -a 'ATI')
atcmd 'AT+CVERSION';  # Show firmware version
atcmd 'AT+QGMR?';     # Show extended firmware version
atcmd 'ATE1';         # Enable the "echo" mode or use 'ATE0' to disable it
atcmd 'AT&W';         # Save all current settings to non-volatile memory (retains them after reboot)

# PDP Context Management
atcmd 'AT+CGDCONT?';      # Show defined PDP contexts
atcmd 'AT+CGACT?';        # Show active PDP contexts
atcmd 'AT+CGACT=0,1';     # Deactivate PDP context 1
atcmd 'AT+CGATT?';        # Check if modem is attached to network

# SIM Status
atcmd 'AT+CPIN?';                 # Check SIM status (READY, PIN required, etc.)
atcmd 'AT+CCID';                  # Show SIM CCID (only works if SIM is inserted)
atcmd 'AT+CLCK="SC",0,"1234"';    # Disable SIM PIN (replace 1234 with actual PIN)
atcmd 'AT+CLCK="SC",1,"1234"';    # Enable SIM PIN
atcmd 'AT+CPWD="SC","OLD","NEW"'; # Change SIM PIN

# Network Information
atcmd 'AT+CGPADDR=1';     # Show current IP address
atcmd 'AT+CSQ';           # Show signal quality [ atcmd -d 500 -a 'AT+CSQ'; # Delay 500ms ]
atcmd 'AT+QCSQ';          # Show signal quality (RSRP, SINR, RSRQ) - format: +QCSQ: "LTE",<rssi>,<rsrp>,<sinr>,<rsrq>
atcmd 'AT+QRSRP';         # Show RSRP signal level
atcmd 'AT+QSINR';         # Show SINR value (format: <pcc>,<scc1>,<scc2>,<scc3>)
atcmd 'AT+CEREG?';        # Check LTE network registration
atcmd 'AT+COPS?';         # Query current registered operator and access technology (7=LTE, 2=UMTS, 0=GSM)
atcmd 'AT+QNWINFO';       # Show current network info (mode, operator, band, channel)

# Primary Cell Info
atcmd 'AT+QENG="servingcell"';      # Show serving cell info
atcmd 'AT+QNETINFO="servingcell"';  # Show current network info

# Neighboring Cells Info
atcmd 'AT+QENG="neighbourcell"';    # Show neighbouring cells

# Carrier Aggregation
atcmd 'AT+QCAINFO';                 # Show carrier aggregation info (LTE only), Requires active data traffic; otherwise returns "OK" only

# SMS Storage / Voice Function
atcmd 'AT+CPMS?';                                # Show current SMS storage
atcmd 'AT+CPMS="ME","ME","ME"'; atcmd 'AT+CSAS'; # Set and save SMS storage to ME
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="call_control",1,1';              # Disable voice call support

# Network Scan Mode (RAT selection)
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="nwscanmode"';       # Show current configuration
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="nwscanmode",0,1';   # Restore automatic mode (LTE/WCDMA) - immediate effect
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="nwscanmode",2,1';   # Force WCDMA (3G) only - immediate effect, no reboot required
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="nwscanmode",3,1';   # Force LTE only - immediate effect, no reboot required

# LTE Bands
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",0,5,0,1';                # Use LTE bands 1+3 only
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",0,45,0,1';               # Use LTE bands 1+3+7 only
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",0,80045,0,1';            # Use LTE bands 1+3+7+20 only
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",0,8000005,0,1';          # Use LTE bands 1+3+28 only
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",8d0,1A0080800D5,0,1';    # Enable all supported LTE bands (EG18-EA)

# USB / Network Interface
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="usbnet"';                             # Show USB network type (0=qmi, 1=ecm, 2=mbim) ** WARNING: mode 3=RNDIS is NOT supported on Linux, only Windows **
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="usbcfg",0x2C7C,0x0512,1,1,1,1,1,1,0'; # Enable ADB interface

# Modem Control
atcmd 'AT+QTEMP';        # Show modem temperature
atcmd 'AT+QPOWD=1';      # Power off the LTE modem - WARNING: modem will NOT restart automatically, may cause permanent loss of remote connectivity if no watchdog is in place
atcmd 'AT+CFUN=1,1';     # Hard reboot modem and enable radio (full reset). 
                         # WARNING: hard reset removes any cell-lock as a side effect 
                         #          (cell-locks are volatile in modem RAM).
                         # WARNING: only for emergency scenarios; regular recovery 
                         #          should use atcmd -C or -S instead.

========================================================================================
STANDARD AT+QNWLOCK COMMANDS
========================================================================================

atcmd 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"';            # Show current cell lock status
atcmd 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",0';          # Disable cell lock without check or fallback
atcmd 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",1,1650,65';  # Single cell lock without check or fallback

========================================================================================
SMART QNWLOCK EXAMPLES (-S)
========================================================================================

# SMART QNWLOCK performs the following steps automatically:
# Setting a lock (value 1-9):
# 1. Sends the AT command passed by the user (no preventive query).
# 2. Restarts modem via soft CFUN (AT+CFUN=0/1).
# 3. Restarts WAN interface.
#    A modem sanity check (AT+CIMI) follows; if it fails, the restart is repeated once.
# 4. Checks LTE connectivity via standard diagnostics.
# 5. On failure, fallback inside atcmd -S:
#    - Removes the cell-lock (AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",0)
#    - Restarts modem and WAN again and checks connectivity again
#    - If it still fails, exits with rc=12
# Removing a lock (value 0): first queries AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"; the procedure above
# (with the same fallback) runs only if a lock is active. If no lock is present, it
# returns rc=13 without consuming CFUN budget. If the query gets no answer after
# 2 attempts (the second with a longer delay), it returns rc=12 and sends nothing.

# Smart QNWLOCK examples
atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"';                     # Execute standard command only
atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",0';                   # Execute SMART only if cell-locks are active

atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",1,100,65';            # Monte Bondone Cima Palon 230131 band 1
atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",2,1650,65,1500,65';   # Monte Bondone Cima Palon 230131 band 3

========================================================================================
SMART CFUN EXAMPLES (-C)
========================================================================================

# SMART CFUN performs the following steps automatically:
# 1. Restarts modem via soft CFUN (AT+CFUN=0/1)
#    NOTE: Soft reset preserves existing cell-lock configuration
# 2. Restarts WAN interface.
# 3. Runs a modem sanity check (AT+CIMI); if it fails, the restart is repeated once.
# 4. Checks LTE connectivity via standard diagnostics.
# 5. On failure, restarts modem and WAN once more (fallback) and checks again.
# 6. Returns 0 on success, 11 if daily limit reached (checked before starting),
#    12 on failure (also if the limit is reached during the procedure).
# A failed run therefore uses at least 4 CFUN operations of the daily budget.

# Smart CFUN is used for general connectivity recovery without 
# touching cell-lock configuration. Use -C when cell-lock 
# management is not needed (remove_cell_lock=0 in watchdog_mbim).

# Smart CFUN examples
atcmd -C                    # Soft CFUN restart, preserve cell-lock
atcmd -C                    # Can be repeated (respects daily limit)

========================================================================================
HARD RESET vs SOFT RESET
========================================================================================

AT+CFUN=1,1 (Hard Reset):
  - Full modem reboot
  - Clears modem RAM state
  - Removes cell-locks (volatile, not NV memory)
  - Reloads firmware from flash
  - Use only in emergencies (watchdog escalation does NOT use this)

AT+CFUN=0 + AT+CFUN=1 (Soft Reset):
  - Radio power cycle only
  - Preserves modem RAM (NVRAM untouched, cell-locks preserved)
  - Standard recovery sequence in atcmd -C and atcmd -S

The watchdog_mbim daemon uses soft reset (-C and -S) for regular recovery.
Hard reset (AT+CFUN=1,1) is NOT used in normal escalation flows.
The last escalation stage of the watchdog is a hardware power cycle through GPIO, which also clears
cell-locks (verified on Quectel EG18-EA).

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# AT+QCFG="nwscanmodeex" bitmask (range 1-63):
#   Bit 0 (1)  = GSM
#   Bit 3 (8)  = WCDMA/UMTS
#   Bit 4 (16) = LTE
#   24 = 16+8  = LTE + WCDMA (factory default, optimal for Europe)
#   63         = all bits set (all technologies enabled)
#
# Relationship between nwscanmodeex and nwscanmode (they are synchronized):
#   nwscanmode=0 (Auto)          nwscanmodeex=24 (LTE+WCDMA)
#   nwscanmode=2 (WCDMA only)    nwscanmodeex=8
#   nwscanmode=3 (LTE only)      nwscanmodeex=16
#
# nwscanmodeex accepts only one parameter (no effect/save flag)
# nwscanmode accepts effect flag: AT+QCFG="nwscanmode",<val>,<0|1>
#
# Changing nwscanmodeex requires inactive PDP context (AT+CGACT=0,1)
# Workaround: use nwscanmode instead, which allows changes with active connection
#
# To restore factory default (nwscanmodeex=24):
#   Step 1 - set all technologies: AT+QCFG="nwscanmodeex",63
#   Step 2 - set Auto mode:        AT+QCFG="nwscanmode",0
#   Result: nwscanmodeex resets to 24 (LTE+WCDMA) automatically

atcmd 'AT+QCFG="nwscanmodeex"';          # expected output: 24 (LTE+WCDMA, factory default)
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="nwscanmode",0';          # set Auto (LTE+WCDMA) equivalent to nwscanmodeex=24
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="nwscanseq",0501030402';  # scan sequence: EVDO>GSM>LTE>CDMA>WCDMA
